/*  What a wire is carrying — and it is not a bit.

    A two-valued signal cannot model hardware. It has no way to say "nobody has driven this
    line yet", no way to say "this line is parked so somebody else may drive it", and no way
    to say "two drivers disagree and the voltage is whatever physics decided". So this
    simulator uses the four values Verilog uses:

        0   driven low
        1   driven high
        x   unknown — uninitialised, or contended
        z   high impedance — floating, not driven at all

    ⚠⚠ THE TABLES BELOW COME FROM VERILOG, NOT FROM REASONING ABOUT UNCERTAINTY. That
    distinction is the whole reason they are worth writing down. A plausible-sounding
    "unknown infects everything" rule gets `0 and x` wrong: the answer is 0, because a
    grounded input pins an AND gate low no matter what the other input turns out to be.
    Likewise `1 or x` is 1. Those two entries are the ones a from-scratch derivation misses,
    and they are exactly the ones that make four-valued simulation useful — without them a
    single uninitialised wire would turn a whole circuit to x and tell you nothing.

    ★ These tables are an EXTERNAL answer key. Nobody on this project invented them; they
    are in the Verilog standard, and anyone can check this file against it. Most of the
    corpus pins whatever the program happened to print, which proves agreement rather than
    correctness. This proves a little more.
*/

// ── The four values ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A closed union of four, so every `Judge` over a signal is checked for coverage by the
// compiler rather than by a reader. Adding a fifth value would make every table below fail
// to compile, which is the correct amount of noise for that change.
//
// ★ They carry no fields. A signal is which of four things it is, and nothing else — a
// `level` number as a field would let `a new signal { the level 9 }` exist, and then every
// table would need a case for a value hardware has no name for.

Define object low with ().
Define object high with ().
Define object unknown with ().
Define object floating with ().

// ── Levels: the tables are data, so a signal needs an index ───────────────
//
// 1..4 rather than 0..3 because Cufet series are 1-based, and an index that needs adjusting
// at every use is an off-by-one waiting to happen.

Bind number to level, given (the (low or high or unknown or floating) wire-value):
    Judge wire-value, where it is:
        A low, return 1.
        A high, return 2.
        An unknown, return 3.
        A floating, return 4.
    Done.
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to at-level, given (the number index):
    If index is 1, return a new low { }.
    If index is 2, return a new high { }.
    If index is 4, return a new floating { }.
    Return a new unknown { }.
Done.

Bind text to shown, given (the (low or high or unknown or floating) wire-value):
    Judge wire-value, where it is:
        A low, return "0".
        A high, return "1".
        An unknown, return "x".
        A floating, return "z".
    Done.
Done.

// ── The tables ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Row-major over (left, right), each in the order 0, 1, x, z — so the entry for a pair is
// at (left - 1) * 4 + right. Written as four lines of four so the shape on the page is the
// shape of the table, and a transcription error shows up as a crooked row.
//
// ⚠ `permanently` is not decoration. A plain `Define` is a VALUE BINDING, local to its
// file; only a permanent one is a constant the rest of this directory can see. These are
// read by `gates.cufe`, so they have to be permanent to exist there at all.

//        0  1  x  z
Define and-table as a series of number with (
    1, 1, 1, 1,     // 0 and _  →  0    ★ a grounded input wins outright
    1, 2, 3, 3,     // 1 and _
    1, 3, 3, 3,     // x and _
    1, 3, 3, 3)     // z and _  →  a floating input reads as unknown
    permanently.

//        0  1  x  z
Define or-table as a series of number with (
    1, 2, 3, 3,     // 0 or _
    2, 2, 2, 2,     // 1 or _   →  2    ★ a driven-high input wins outright
    3, 2, 3, 3,     // x or _
    3, 2, 3, 3)     // z or _
    permanently.

//        0  1  x  z
Define xor-table as a series of number with (
    1, 2, 3, 3,     // 0 xor _
    2, 1, 3, 3,     // 1 xor _
    3, 3, 3, 3,     // x xor _  →  xor has no dominating input, so x spreads
    3, 3, 3, 3)     // z xor _
    permanently.

//        0  1  x  z
Define not-table as a series of number with (2, 1, 3, 3) permanently.

/*  Wire resolution — what a line carries when SEVERAL gates drive it.

    ★ This is the table that makes `z` earn its place, and it is not one of the logic
    operations. Two drivers on one wire is a hardware fact, not an expression: a bus works
    precisely because every driver but one is parked at z, and z loses to everything.

        z with anything  →  the anything      (parked drivers yield)
        0 with 0         →  0                 (drivers agreeing is fine)
        0 with 1         →  x                 ⚠ CONTENTION — and in real hardware, smoke
        z with z         →  z                 (nobody is driving at all)
*/
//        0  1  x  z
Define resolve-table as a series of number with (
    1, 3, 3, 1,     // 0 with _
    3, 2, 3, 2,     // 1 with _
    3, 3, 3, 3,     // x with _
    1, 2, 3, 4)     // z with _  →  z yields to every real driver
    permanently.

// ── Reading a table ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to paired, given (
        the series of number table,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Define row as cast level on (left).
    Define column as cast level on (right).
    Return cast at-level on (item ((row - 1) * 4 + column) of table).
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to both-of, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Return cast paired on (and-table, left, right).
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to either-of, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Return cast paired on (or-table, left, right).
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to differing, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Return cast paired on (xor-table, left, right).
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to inverted, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) wire-value):
    Return cast at-level on (item (cast level on (wire-value)) of not-table).
Done.

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to resolved, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Return cast paired on (resolve-table, left, right).
Done.

Bind fact to same-signal, given (
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) left,
        the (low or high or unknown or floating) right):
    Return cast level on (left) is cast level on (right).
Done.

// ── Reading a signal from a person ────────────────────────────────────────

Bind (low or high or unknown or floating) to from-digit, given (the number digit):
    If digit is 0, return a new low { }.
    If digit is 1, return a new high { }.
    Return a new unknown { }.
Done.
